8. Privacy, Security and Reliability
8.1 Privacy
The initial version uses hosted Gemma inference.
This means extracted document text sent for AI processing is transmitted to the configured inference service. The interface and documentation must disclose this clearly.
Do not claim that AI processing is entirely offline or that documents never leave the user's device.
Prefer local storage for the user's persistent library and avoid retaining document content on the backend beyond what is necessary to process a request.
The app must explain which data is stored locally and which content is sent to the inference provider.
8.2 Security
* Never expose the model API key in frontend code.
* Store secrets in the supported server-side secret configuration.
* Validate uploaded files and impose sensible size limits.
* Do not execute uploaded content.
* Prevent unsafe rendering of document content.
* Do not trust model-generated HTML or code.
* Handle errors without exposing secrets or internal stack traces to users.
* Avoid logging full document contents.
* Provide a way to clear the stored library.
8.3 Reliability
The application must:
* Handle empty documents.
* Handle corrupted or unsupported files.
* Handle API timeouts and rate limits.
* Handle invalid model responses.
* Allow retries after recoverable failures.
* Preserve successfully extracted text if indexing fails.
* Prevent a single failed document from breaking the whole upload batch.
* Display meaningful error messages.
8.4 Accessibility
* Support keyboard navigation.
* Provide accessible names for interactive controls.
* Use readable text sizes and adequate contrast.
* Provide visible focus states.
* Avoid relying exclusively on color for status or meaning.
* Ensure core functionality works on desktop and mobile-sized screens.
________________


9. Non-Functional Requirements
Performance
* The interface should render without waiting for AI processing.
* Search should operate over the indexed library rather than repeatedly uploading all documents.
* Show progress for long-running operations.
* Limit the amount of source text sent to the model to what is necessary.
* Use pagination or incremental rendering if the library becomes large.
Maintainability
* Keep model integration separate from UI components.
* Separate document parsing, indexing, search, and presentation logic.
* Use typed interfaces and schema validation.
* Keep provider credentials and configuration out of the frontend.
* Write a clear README with setup instructions.
Portability
* Keep the code exportable from Google AI Studio.
* Avoid unnecessary dependence on proprietary UI components.
* Make the model adapter replaceable.
* Document the required environment variables and deployment assumptions.
________________


10. MVP Acceptance Criteria
The MVP is ready for demonstration only when the following criteria pass:
1. A user can upload supported TXT, Markdown, and text-based PDF files.
2. The backend extracts usable text from supported documents.
3. Gemma generates real document summaries and topic metadata.
4. Generated metadata is validated before being saved.
5. A user can search for a concept without knowing the exact filename.
6. Search returns relevant documents with understandable explanations.
7. Displayed direct quotations are verified against the extracted source text.
8. The application can show a useful empty result when no matching document is found.
9. Users can inspect document summaries and related information.
10. Users can create or use suggested collections.
11. The library persists across refreshes in the same browser.
12. Failed indexing does not erase already extracted text.
13. API keys are not exposed in client-side code.
14. The application clearly explains its hosted inference and privacy behavior.
15. The core workflow functions without relying on mock AI responses.
Suggested evaluation
Prepare a small, fixed test library of 10–15 documents covering different topics and overlapping terminology.
Evaluate:
* Whether relevant documents appear in search results.
* Whether the correct document is ranked highly.
* Whether source quotations are valid.
* Whether summaries are faithful to the source.
* Whether irrelevant files are incorrectly presented as strong matches.
* How long indexing and search take.
* How the app behaves when a document is incomplete or contradictory.
Record actual results rather than inventing performance claims.
